---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'tldr', label: 'TL;DR' },
  { id: 'problem', label: 'Problem' },
  { id: 'architecture', label: 'Architecture' },
  { id: 'identity', label: 'Identity' },
  { id: 'negotiation', label: 'Negotiation' },
  { id: 'delivery', label: 'Delivery' },
  { id: 'role', label: 'My role' },
];

const stats = [
  { v: '11', l: 'microservices (from 1 monolith)' },
  { v: '27', l: 'tracked tickets shipped' },
  { v: '< 4 mo', l: 'to deliver them' },
  { v: '15+', l: 'PRs across legacy + modern code' },
];
---

<Base
  title="Venue Network — case study"
  description="Migrating a legacy event-ticketing monolith to Node.js microservices: identity from scratch, a multi-party offer-negotiation system, and production data infrastructure."
>
  <header class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 pb-8 pt-16 sm:px-8 sm:pt-20">
    <p class="eyebrow reveal">Case study · production systems</p>
    <h1
      class="reveal mt-4 max-w-3xl text-4xl font-semibold leading-[1.08] tracking-tight sm:text-5xl"
    >
      Venue Network: taking apart a ticketing monolith without dropping it
    </h1>
    <p class="reveal mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
      Backend &amp; DevOps contract work on a live event-management and ticketing
      platform — the systems-engineering counterpart to the
      <a href="/soilmetrix" class="text-moss-600 underline decoration-moss-300 underline-offset-2 hover:text-moss-700">SoilMetrix ML pipeline</a>.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-12 px-5 sm:px-8 lg:grid-cols-[200px_1fr]">
    <aside class="hidden lg:block">
      <nav class="sticky top-20 space-y-1.5 border-l border-line pl-4">
        {
          toc.map((t) => (
            <a
              href={`#${t.id}`}
              class="block py-0.5 text-sm text-ink-3 transition-colors hover:text-moss-600"
            >
              {t.label}
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </nav>
    </aside>

    <article class="min-w-0">
      <!-- TL;DR -->
      <section id="tldr" class="scroll-mt-20">
        <div class="reveal rounded-2xl border border-moss-200 bg-moss-50/50 p-6 sm:p-8">
          <p class="eyebrow">TL;DR</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-[1.05rem] leading-relaxed text-ink">
            I led the migration of a legacy monolith into
            <span class="tnum font-medium text-moss-700">11</span> Node.js/PostgreSQL
            microservices on Render, shipping
            <span class="tnum font-medium text-moss-700">27</span> tracked tickets in
            under four months — including the platform's identity service built from
            scratch (hierarchical RBAC, OAuth&nbsp;2.0, step-up auth) and the data
            models for a turn-based, multi-party offer-negotiation system with
            version control.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="reveal mt-5 grid grid-cols-2 gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-line bg-line sm:grid-cols-4">
          {
            stats.map((s) => (
              <div class="bg-card px-4 py-5">
                <p class="tnum text-2xl font-semibold text-moss-700">{s.v}</p>
                <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-[0.72rem] leading-snug text-ink-3">{s.l}</p>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- PROBLEM -->
      <section id="problem" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-16">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">01 · Problem</p>
        <h2 class="reveal mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
          A live platform, a legacy core
        </h2>
        <div class="prose-col mt-5 space-y-4 text-[1.02rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          <p class="reveal">
            Venue Network runs event management and ticketing — inventory,
            artist-booking workflows, settlements — for real venues, on a codebase
            that had outgrown its monolith. The mandate: decompose it into services
            a small team can evolve independently, while the existing product keeps
            serving traffic. That means every change lands twice — carved out of the
            legacy code and rebuilt cleanly — which is why PRs span both codebases.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ARCHITECTURE -->
      <section id="architecture" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-16">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">02 · Architecture</p>
        <h2 class="reveal mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
          Eleven services behind one gateway
        </h2>
        <div class="prose-col mt-5 space-y-4 text-[1.02rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          <p class="reveal">
            The target architecture is Node.js microservices — organized in an NX
            monorepo, each with its own PostgreSQL schema — behind a Kong API
            gateway, deployed on Render. I provisioned the production PostgreSQL and
            Redis infrastructure, and built supporting pipelines like event-poster
            thumbnail processing (Imagick/WebP). Load behavior is verified with k6
            rather than assumed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- IDENTITY -->
      <section id="identity" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-16">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">03 · Identity</p>
        <h2 class="reveal mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
          Identity, built from scratch
        </h2>
        <div class="prose-col mt-5 space-y-4 text-[1.02rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          <p class="reveal">
            Ticketing has an unusual identity problem: venues, promoters, artists and
            buyers all touch the same objects with very different rights, and third-party
            sites embed the ticketing widget. I built the platform's identity service
            from scratch: <span class="font-medium text-ink">hierarchical RBAC</span> so
            permissions follow organizational structure, email verification,
            <span class="font-medium text-ink">step-up authentication</span> for
            sensitive actions, and <span class="font-medium text-ink">OAuth&nbsp;2.0</span>
            so the embeddable widget can authenticate safely on other people's domains.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- NEGOTIATION -->
      <section id="negotiation" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-16">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">04 · Negotiation</p>
        <h2 class="reveal mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
          Offers as versioned state machines
        </h2>
        <div class="prose-col mt-5 space-y-4 text-[1.02rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          <p class="reveal">
            Booking an artist is a negotiation: offers pass between multiple parties,
            each revision changes terms, and everyone must sign the version they
            actually agreed to. I designed the data models for a
            <span class="font-medium text-ink">turn-based offer-negotiation system</span>
            with multi-party signing and full version control — every state of an offer
            is preserved, so "what did we agree to, and when" is a query, not an
            argument.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- DELIVERY -->
      <section id="delivery" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-16">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">05 · Delivery</p>
        <h2 class="reveal mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
          Contract pace, tracked in the open
        </h2>
        <div class="prose-col mt-5 space-y-4 text-[1.02rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          <p class="reveal">
            27 tracked tickets shipped in under four months, alongside 15+ pull
            requests spanning the legacy and modern codebases — while holding the
            SoilMetrix role and an MS courseload concurrently. Client names and
            proprietary internals are omitted here by design; the architecture
            decisions and delivery record are the demonstrable part.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ROLE -->
      <section id="role" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-16">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal">06 · My role</p>
        <h2 class="reveal mt-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-3xl">
          Backend &amp; DevOps, contract
        </h2>
        <div class="prose-col mt-5 space-y-4 text-[1.02rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          <p class="reveal">
            Software Engineer (Backend &amp; DevOps), November 2025 – present. I led
            the monolith decomposition and own the identity service, the
            offer-negotiation data models, and the production data infrastructure.
            Frontend, product direction, and the booking-intelligence ML model
            involve the wider team.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
